<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caen is a city on the River <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orne-river">Orne</a> in Normandy, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It grew under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>, who built its castle and, with his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-flanders">Matilda of Flanders</a>, founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-etienne-caen">Abbaye aux Hommes</a> and the Abbaye aux Dames in the 1060s; it later housed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exchequer-of-normandy">Exchequer of Normandy</a>. Its limestone was shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> for churches and castles, and much of the city was destroyed in the Battle of Normandy in 1944.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caen.jpg" alt="Caen" width="320" height="494" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TTaylor · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:France_Caen_Trinite_c.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Roman times the site was known as Catumagos, from Gaulish words for combat and field, and it remained a minor settlement. In 912 Caen became the western capital of the Duchy of Normandy, and the patronage of the dukes brought a major development of commerce in the 10th century. Around 1060 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> began the Château de Caen, one of the largest medieval fortresses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, which became the centre of the ducal court.</p><p>His wife, Duchess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-flanders">Matilda of Flanders</a>, founded the Benedictine Abbey of Sainte-Trinité at about the same time and was buried in it. Caen succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a> as capital of Lower Normandy, beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> as the second ducal capital, and William was buried in the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a> took it on 21 May 1204, and it passed with the rest of Normandy into the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>During the Hundred Years' War Edward III of England stormed and sacked the city on 26 July 1346, in less than a day, killing 3,000 citizens and burning much of the merchants' quarter on the Île St-Jean; only the castle held out. Henry V captured it after a siege in 1417 and treated it harshly as the first town to resist his invasion. French forces recaptured it in 1450. The University of Caen was founded in 1432 by John of Lancaster, Duke of Bedford, and Henry VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><p>German forces occupied Caen after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-france">Battle of France</a> in 1940. After the Allied landings of Operation Overlord, the British Second Army fought the Battle of Caen from 6 June to 6 August 1944 to dislodge German forces. Allied bombing raids heavily damaged the city and caused many French civilian casualties, and little of prewar Caen remained. Reconstruction of whole districts and the university campus took fourteen years, from 1948 to 1962. A Canadian Army Film and Photo Unit recorded the recovery in the film You Can't Kill a City. The city has since built the Mémorial de Caen, a museum dedicated to peace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Caen is the prefecture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvados">Calvados</a>, on the River Orne about 15 km from the coast and 200 km northwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. The city proper has 105,512 inhabitants. A canal built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> links it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> at Ouistreham. It remains known for the buildings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>'s reign, including the castle and the abbeys.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Caen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Normandy was one of the most fertile and densely settled regions of northern France, with grain lands in the Pays de Caux and the plain of <b>Caen</b>, pasture in the Pays d’Auge and the Cotentin, and the Seine as its main highway.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Known from Pliny and Ptolemy, the Viducasses lived south of modern <b>Caen</b> in lower Normandy.</blockquote><a href="/state/viducasses">Viducasses</a></li></ul></section>
